WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.448
Protocol-aware telemetry adds industrial meaning to network communication.

00:00:03.448 --> 00:00:09.307
Instead of reporting only addresses and ports, it may identify device roles, sessions, reads,

00:00:09.307 --> 00:00:12.236
writes, commands, errors, discovery, or engineering activity.

00:00:12.236 --> 00:00:16.839
This additional context helps teams distinguish routine control from meaningful change.

00:00:16.839 --> 00:00:21.861
Modbus telemetry can separate common reads from writes and identify expected clients.

00:00:21.861 --> 00:00:26.046
DNP3 visibility may describe master and outstation relationships, event reporting,

00:00:26.046 --> 00:00:27.302
and control operations.

00:00:27.302 --> 00:00:31.905
OPC UA sources can expose application sessions, certificate events, security policy,

00:00:31.905 --> 00:00:32.742
and subscriptions.

00:00:32.742 --> 00:00:36.927
EtherNet/IP and PROFINET environments may include cyclic control traffic, diagnostics,

00:00:36.927 --> 00:00:39.019
discovery, configuration, and engineering functions.

00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:43.204
Expected patterns depend on the vendor ecosystem and engineered design.

00:00:43.204 --> 00:00:48.226
A new peer or unusual management operation may deserve review even when

00:00:48.226 --> 00:00:50.318
the protocol itself is approved.

00:00:50.318 --> 00:00:52.829
Protocol labels are not process meaning.

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:57.014
A write may be normal during startup, approved during maintenance,

00:00:57.014 --> 00:00:58.688
and concerning during production.

00:00:58.688 --> 00:01:02.036
Register, object, and tag semantics vary across sites.

00:01:02.036 --> 00:01:04.547
Asset role, process state, user authority,

00:01:04.547 --> 00:01:08.732
and work records provide the context telemetry cannot infer alone.

00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:11.661
Encrypted or proprietary communication can limit inspection.

00:01:11.661 --> 00:01:15.846
Do not weaken secure settings simply to collect more content.

00:01:15.846 --> 00:01:19.613
Endpoint logs, gateway records, controller change records, certificate events,

00:01:19.613 --> 00:01:23.379
and session metadata can fill part of the gap.

00:01:23.379 --> 00:01:27.145
Document parser coverage, protocol versions, unsupported operations, and confidence.

00:01:27.145 --> 00:01:31.749
Protocol-aware telemetry is valuable when it explains behavior without overstating intent.

00:01:31.749 --> 00:01:36.771
It should help an analyst ask better questions and engage the engineer

00:01:36.771 --> 00:01:39.700
or operator who can determine operational consequence.
